WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.340
CARTER MORGAN: It's time to
look at the Kubernetes API

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.880
and how it makes modeling the
application lifecycle easier.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.590
With a whole universe
of things to learn,

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.870
it's important to start
with the essentials.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.370
Let's go over the
concepts that make

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.640
Kubernetes usable, scalable,
and just downright awesome.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.390
Are you ready?

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.240
Kaslin, when it comes to what
scalable applications need,

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.280
we've talked about
containers and nodes,

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.180
but that's just the
tip of the iceberg.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.810
There's plenty more we need in
order to run a full scalable

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:26.710
application.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.450
KASLIN FIELDS:
Exactly, and that's

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.820
where the Kubernetes
API comes in.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.860
It offers some really
convenient primitives

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:36.850
that make managing cloud native
applications a lot easier.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.250
We've already talked
about a few API objects,

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:43.600
like pods and nodes, but there's
also services, deployments,

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.883
secrets, and many more.

00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:47.050
CARTER MORGAN: That's a lot.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.695
Why don't we start simple and
use an example application?

00:00:50.695 --> 00:00:53.320
We said that containers are the
first step to using Kubernetes,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.850
so let's start
with this container

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.140
I built to run my Hello app.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.340
This app is really simple.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.160
It just returns "Hello"
whenever I ping it

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.820
on its local IP in port 8080.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.047
So what next?

00:01:04.047 --> 00:01:06.130
KASLIN FIELDS: Well, the
first thing we have to do

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.410
is to create some
machines, or nodes,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:09.732
to run your application on.

00:01:09.732 --> 00:01:10.690
CARTER MORGAN: Got you.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.050
OK, I'll use Google Kubernetes
Engine to quickly get started.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.360
I can just use the
gcloud command line tool

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.162
to provision a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:19.870
After a few minutes,
we'll have a cluster

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:24.040
that's all ready to go, and by
default comes with three nodes.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.890
This is a great starting
point, but now we

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:28.880
need to actually run my app.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.147
So how do we do that?

00:01:30.147 --> 00:01:32.230
KASLIN FIELDS: Since we're
using the command line,

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.950
we can use a handy tool called
kubectl to help interact

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.840
with the Kubernetes API.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.230
Don't worry, we'll go into
kubectl in more detail later.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:42.820
But we're keeping
it simple, remember?

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.945
CARTER MORGAN: But what
about all these other tools

00:01:44.945 --> 00:01:48.200
I've heard of, kubecontrol,
kubecuddle, kube-ectl?

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.200
KASLIN FIELDS: There are
a lot of different ways

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.130
to pronounce the
name of this tool,

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:53.950
so whatever works for you.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.260
Anyway, this command
is actually going

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.175
to create a Kubernetes API
object called a deployment.

00:02:00.175 --> 00:02:02.050
CARTER MORGAN: A deployment
is an abstraction

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.300
that manages the life
cycle of an application.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.310
I can set the desired
number of app instances

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.050
for the deployment to
manage, and then it

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.120
will make sure the correct
number of instances,

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.412
or replicas, are running.

00:02:11.412 --> 00:02:12.370
KASLIN FIELDS: Exactly.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.760
So if we increase the number
of replicas that we want,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.860
the deployment will see
that there's currently not

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.590
enough replicas and
spin up another one.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.810
CARTER MORGAN: That even
works when a node crashes.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:24.820
If the node goes down,
the current state

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.460
is once again different
from the desired state,

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.760
and Kubernetes will schedule
another replica for us.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:31.510
Awesome.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.370
Now I know my app is
running on these nodes.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.010
How do I access it?

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.830
KASLIN FIELDS: For that, we'll
have to create a service.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.660
This creates an
endpoint that we can

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.400
use to access the
running app instances.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:47.060
In this case, we have
multiple app instances.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.780
So this service will load
balance incoming requests

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.330
between the two running pods.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:53.500
CARTER MORGAN: That's right.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.720
For any container
inside of the cluster,

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:59.000
they can connect to my service
using the service's name.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.730
Either way, the
service keeps track

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.038
of wherever the pod is running.

00:03:02.038 --> 00:03:03.580
KASLIN FIELDS: That's
another example

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.230
of how Kubernetes
removes the need

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.290
to manually keep track of where
your containers are running.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:11.320
Even if a pod were to go down,
once a new one comes back

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.240
online, the service
will automatically

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.300
update its list of endpoints
to target the new pod.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.610
CARTER MORGAN: So Kubernetes
objects, like deployments

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.770
and services,
automatically ensure

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.020
that we have the right number
of app instances running

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.020
through pods, and that
we can always reach them.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:29.188
This seems like it would allow
for some really cool features.

00:03:29.188 --> 00:03:29.980
KASLIN FIELDS: Yes.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.680
Features that used to
have to be manually coded

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.440
just become afterthoughts
when using Kubernetes.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.890
For example, deployments make
things like rolling updates

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:39.873
really simple.

00:03:39.873 --> 00:03:41.290
CARTER MORGAN:
Whoa, you're saying

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.330
that we can edit the
deployment and watch

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.700
the version of the
applications gradually change?

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.670
So let's say we
have three replicas,

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.760
and I want to put out a new
version of my application

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.890
that returns "Bye"
instead of "Hello."

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.440
You're saying I can update
my application container,

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.590
watch as the new version
is gradually rolled out,

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:02.980
and the deployment will bring
up new application instances

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:04.900
and start rerouting
traffic to them?

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.240
Then once the desired number
of new instances are online,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.980
the old application
instances are taken offline?

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.890
This would mean this
approach has zero downtime,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.709
since Kubernetes is
incrementally updating old pod

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:19.022
instances with new ones.

00:04:19.022 --> 00:04:20.230
KASLIN FIELDS: Exactly right.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.780
And a feature like
that, plus the ability

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:25.030
to quickly roll
back if necessary,

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:28.720
and keeping track of deployment
histories and more, all of that

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.700
is just built into Kubernetes.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.070
That's a large part of what
makes it such a great tool

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:36.023
to build other systems and
applications on top of.

00:04:36.023 --> 00:04:37.690
CARTER MORGAN: The
Kubernetes API really

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.630
does make it easy to manage
the application lifecycle.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.420
The basic primitives--
podds, service, deployments,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.280
plus a few more we'll
talk about soon--

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.430
allow sysadmins and
developers to focus on the app

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:51.280
without having to worry
about managing it at scale.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.940
But one thing I noticed is that
we used an imperative approach.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.010
We made manual commands,
instead of using

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:58.840
a declarative
approach, which is one

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.640
of the big appeals
of Kubernetes.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:01.990
KASLIN FIELDS: Great catch.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.420
Next episode, let's
dig into that.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.420
We'll use Kubernetes the way
it was intended to be used,

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.730
by updating config files.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:11.710
Starting with an
example monolith,

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.870
we'll break it up into
a microservices style

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:14.800
architecture.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.320
This will also
let introduce some

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.690
of the other core primitives
that we didn't talk about,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.055
like labels and secrets.

00:05:21.055 --> 00:05:22.680
CARTER MORGAN: We're
going to pair code

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.060
up a microservice
version of this?

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:25.560
Hm.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.540
OK, stay tuned, everybody.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.570
Next time, we'll be exploring
building a basic Kubernetes

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:32.370
app from scratch.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.860
In the meantime, if you
want to get hands-on,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.540
check out the link
in the description.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.110
And if you enjoyed the
episode, subscribe for more.

